#!/bin/sh
# Stand in front of every `rustc` this workspace runs and refuse the ones the
# filesystem cannot hold.
#
# WHY THE EXISTING CHECK DOES NOT COVER THIS. `scripts/sandboxed` asks the same
# question with the same numbers, but only when somebody types it, and the
# spelling this repository's own measuring guidance uses is bare:
# `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli`. `.cargo/config.toml`'s runner entry
# cannot help either -- cargo calls a runner to *run* a binary, which is after
# every link has already happened -- and that is why the wrapper skips the
# free-space check in `--cargo-runner` mode. Both recorded incidents were
# builds, so both would still die as an LLVM stack trace today.
#
# A `build.rs` was the other candidate and reaches less than it appears to.
# Cargo caches a build script's result and re-runs it when the package changes,
# so it fires on the from-clean build and not on the one after it; and it lives
# in one package, so `cargo build -p nsh-survey` would never run nsh-cli's.
# `rustc` is where every one of those commands meets the disk.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured 2026-09-04 on this machine at load 21-28, from clean,
# into a private `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`:
#
#   nothing to switch on. Cargo's fingerprint does not include the wrapper, so
#   adding it recompiled nothing: `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli` answered
#   "Finished in 0.11s" without it and "in 0.09s" with it. Same reason turning
#   sccache on does not rebuild the world.
#
#   2.29 ms per invocation, and rustc runs once per crate rather than once per
#   file. 43 invocations for a from-clean `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli`:
#   98 ms against a 20.9 s build, and 99 MiB of output. 150 invocations for a
#   from-clean `cargo test --workspace --no-run`: 344 ms against 33.7 s, and
#   2.9 GiB of output -- which is the figure the 2 GiB threshold sits below.
#
# THE SHELL IS `sh` AND THE `statfs` IS ONE CALL BECAUSE OF THAT ARITHMETIC.
# The same script under `#!/bin/bash` with two `stat` invocations measured
# 6.61 ms, which is a third of a second added to a workspace build to ask a
# question twice in a slower shell.
#
# THE THRESHOLD AND THE MODE COME FROM THE WRAPPER'S OWN VARIABLES, so the two
# checks cannot disagree about what "enough" means or about whether the reader
# has already said to go ahead. `NSH_TEST_DISK=warn` builds here rather than
# warning: the difference between warn and refuse is a message printed once,
# and this runs once per crate, so warning would arrive forty-three times for a
# build that was going to succeed.
#
# IT NEVER REFUSES OVER A QUESTION IT COULD NOT ASK. A path it cannot `statfs`
# is not evidence of a full disk, and a build stopped by a broken check is a
# worse outcome than the failure this exists to name.
set -eu

if [ "$#" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "usage: cargo calls this as build.rustc-wrapper; see .cargo/config.toml" >&2
    exit 2
fi

DISK=${NSH_TEST_DISK:-refuse}
DISK_MIN=${NSH_TEST_DISK_MIN:-2048}
case $DISK in
    refuse) ;;
    warn | ignore) exec "$@" ;;
    *)
        echo "scripts/room-to-build: NSH_TEST_DISK must be refuse, warn or ignore" >&2
        exit 2
        ;;
esac
case $DISK_MIN in
    '' | *[!0-9]*)
        echo "scripts/room-to-build: NSH_TEST_DISK_MIN must be a number of mebibytes" >&2
        exit 2
        ;;
esac

# Where this compilation's output is going, which is the filesystem the
# question is about, and the reason it is read rather than assumed: a
# `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` can sit on a different device from the checkout, and then
# only one of the two answers.
#
# AN INVOCATION WITH NO `--out-dir` IS LET THROUGH RATHER THAN ASKED ABOUT THE
# WORKING DIRECTORY. Cargo omits it for the `rustc -vV` probe it runs at
# startup, which writes nothing; asking about the working directory there would
# refuse a worktree on a full filesystem whose build tree has room, and it
# would do it before cargo had said which crate it was even building.
OUT=
previous=
for argument in "$@"; do
    if [ "$previous" = --out-dir ]; then
        OUT=$argument
        break
    fi
    previous=$argument
done
[ -n "$OUT" ] || exec "$@"

# `%a` is the count of blocks a non-superuser may still use and `%S` the size
# of one, which is the product `df` prints as "Avail" -- the same reading
# `scripts/sandboxed` and `scripts/disk-headroom` take.
room=$(/usr/bin/stat -f -c '%a %S' "$OUT" 2>/dev/null) || exec "$@"
available=$((${room% *} * ${room#* }))
[ "$available" -lt "$((DISK_MIN * 1024 * 1024))" ] || exec "$@"

# The three signatures are quoted here rather than left to a document because
# the reader arrives at this message instead of at one of them, and the next
# thing they do is search for whichever one they saw last time.
/usr/bin/awk -v o="$OUT" -v a="$available" -v m="$((DISK_MIN * 1024 * 1024))" \
    'BEGIN { printf "scripts/room-to-build: %s has %.2f GiB free, under %.2f GiB\n", o, a / 1073741824, m / 1073741824 }' >&2
cat >&2 <<'REASON'
Refusing to compile into it. Below that mark a build's failure stops naming its
own cause: a linker out of room dies as 'ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus
error]' under an LLVM stack trace asking for an upstream bug report, rustc dies
as an ICE carrying no message, and only cargo itself ever says 'No space left
on device'.

  scripts/disk-headroom              # where the space went
  scripts/disk-headroom --reclaim    # take back what is safe to take

NSH_TEST_DISK=ignore builds anyway; NSH_TEST_DISK_MIN is the threshold in
mebibytes.
REASON
exit 1
